1. Power Supply Features
  
  A power supply unit (PSU) converts AC power from the wall outlet into the DC power all computer components actually use, then regulates and
  distributes it to the motherboard, CPU, GPU, and peripherals — each with different power requirements. This applies to desktop-style systems
  only; laptops have their own internal battery and charger. An incorrect or inadequate PSU causes system instability and shortens component
  lifespan.
  
  Wattage: the total power output of the unit (commonly 500W, 750W, 1000W+), sized to match how many components the system has.
  
  Efficiency rating (80 PLUS certification): measures efficiency at varying loads. Levels from lowest to highest: Standard/White, Bronze,
  Silver, Gold, Platinum, Titanium — Titanium can reach 94–95% efficiency. Higher efficiency means less wasted heat and lower power draw.
  
  Form factors: ATX (standard for desktops, ~150mm x 86mm), Small Form Factor (compact builds), TFX and FlexATX (slim and Mini-ITX cases).
  The PSU's form factor must match the case.
  
  Common power connectors: 24-pin ATX (motherboard), 4-pin/8-pin EPS (CPU), 6+2 pin PCIe (GPU/other PCIe devices — the 2-pin section is often
  detachable depending on whether the device needs 6 or 8 pins), and SATA/Molex (storage devices, case fans, peripherals).
  
  Modularity: non-modular (all cables permanently attached, cheapest, more clutter), semi-modular (some fixed, some detachable), and fully
  modular (all cables detachable — best cable management and airflow).
  
  Selection best practices: calculate wattage needs for all components, aim for Gold-level efficiency or higher, verify connector
  compatibility and case fit, and always prefer overpowering to underpowering — unused connectors can simply stay unplugged,
  but insufficient wattage causes instability. Avoid low-efficiency units and don't ignore future expansion needs (extra GPUs, more storage).

2. Power Supply Input
  
  Power supply input refers to the AC voltage range the PSU accepts from the wall outlet — matching this to regional standards is
  essential, since the wrong voltage can damage hardware.
  
  Standard voltage ratings: 110–120V AC at 60Hz is used primarily in North America and Japan (needs higher current for the same power
  output). 220–240V AC at 50Hz is used in most of Europe, Asia, and Australia, delivering power more efficiently with less transmission
  loss — common in high-performance computing and data centers. Step-down adapters can convert 220–240V to 110–120V for travel.
  
  Voltage selection: auto-switching PSUs detect and adjust automatically; manual-switching units have a small slide switch on the back
  (often needs a flathead screwdriver, deliberately not easy to flip by accident).
  
  Input protection: some PSUs have built-in overvoltage/undervoltage and short-circuit protection. Where that's absent, use a separate
  surge protector (evens out incoming power fluctuations) or a UPS (uninterruptible power supply — provides battery backup during outages, and
  many also include surge protection). Both are important for mission-critical systems.
  
  Common mistakes: using an incompatible PSU without a step-down/step-up transformer, forgetting to flip the manual voltage selector
  before first use (can cause irreparable damage), and assuming a physically compatible plug means compatible power delivery — always confirm
  the region's voltage standard.

3. Power Supply Output
  
  Power supply output refers to the specific voltages the PSU delivers to different components via dedicated "rails." Incorrect voltage
  delivery can cause hardware failure or reduced performance.
  
  3.3V rail: powers lower-power components — RAM, the motherboard chipset, and M.2 solid-state drives.
  
  5V rail: powers USB devices, non-M.2 SSDs, and some legacy components; provides stable current for control circuits and logic chips.
  
  12V rail: the main power source for higher-power components — CPU, GPU, fans, and hard drives/optical drives. Critical for stability in
  gaming and high-performance systems.
  
  Single-rail vs. multi-rail (12V): a single 12V rail delivers all 12V power from one source — simpler but a failure impacts the whole system.
  A multi-rail design distributes 12V power across multiple rails for better protection against overload, at a higher cost but with greater
  reliability.
  
  Voltage regulation: higher-end PSUs use Voltage Regulation Modules (VRMs) to stabilize output. Higher efficiency ratings also mean cleaner,
  more stable power for sensitive components, less heat, and lower long-term power draw.
  
  Selection considerations: check CPU/GPU wattage needs, prioritize voltage stability and regulation quality, and verify the 80 PLUS rating.
  Common mistakes: overloading a single rail when a multi-rail unit is actually needed, using low-quality PSUs with poor voltage regulation,
  and ignoring future upgrade power needs.

4. Motherboard Connectors
  
  The main motherboard power connector is the 20+4 pin connector — a 20-pin base plus a separate or slide-together 4-pin segment, together
  forming the standard 24-pin ATX connector for modern motherboards. This design supports both older 20-pin motherboards (the 4-pin segment
  simply stays unused) and newer 24-pin boards. Always check motherboard specs before buying a PSU, since some high-performance boards need
  additional connectors.
  
  Other motherboard-related connectors:
    
    - 4-pin/8-pin CPU power connector — dedicated CPU power, sometimes physically part of the 20+4 configuration, sometimes fully separate;
      pin count depends on CPU make/model.
    - PCIe power connectors — typically 6-pin or 8-pin, for GPUs and other PCIe devices.
    - SATA and Molex power connectors — for hard drives and optical drives. SATA is the modern standard (also used by many SSDs); Molex is
      the legacy 4-pin connector from the IDE era, rarely found in modern systems.
    
  Installation: align the connector using its keyed shape (it can only go in one orientation), ensure a secure fit without forcing it, and
  verify all connections before powering on to avoid shorts.
  
  Troubleshooting: if the connector seems loose, reseat it and confirm the retention clip is fully engaged. No power to the motherboard at
  all → check the PSU itself and cable connections. Instability while running → confirm the PSU can supply sufficient wattage for everything
  connected.
  
  PSU selection for the motherboard: match the wattage rating to total system demand, consider modular vs. non-modular for cable management
  and airflow, and aim for 80 PLUS Gold or higher. Avoid underpowered units, forgetting to connect all required cables, or choosing
  low-quality PSUs with unstable regulation.

5. Power Supply Selection
  
  Choosing the right PSU means matching it to the number and type of connected devices — undersizing risks crashes, overheating, and
  inefficiency, while leaving headroom supports future expansion.
  
  Rough wattage guidelines per component (these vary by make/model and actual load): CPU wattage varies widely by model; GPU needs
  150–450W for high-performance cards; motherboard + RAM together need roughly 50–100W; each storage device (HDD/SSD/optical) needs
  about 10W; each fan needs 5–15W; allocate roughly 25W per additional expansion device (network card, sound card, etc.). As a rule of
  thumb, budgeting toward the higher end of each range leaves comfortable headroom.
  
  System-level wattage recommendations: a basic office PC needs roughly 300–450W; a gaming PC needs roughly 550–850W; a workstation or
  multi-GPU setup needs 1000W or more.
  
  Modularity recap: non-modular (all cables fixed, unused ones just hang loose, can restrict airflow), semi-modular (some fixed, some
  removable), fully modular (all cables detachable, best airflow and cable management).
  
  Future-proofing: budget an extra 150–200W beyond current needs to accommodate future upgrades (e.g., a more powerful GPU later).
  
  Common mistakes: buying a PSU that just barely meets current needs, and not accounting for future component upgrades that will draw
  significantly more power.
  
  Selection summary: calculate total power consumption, add 20–30% extra wattage for safety margin, look for 80 PLUS Gold or
  higher, confirm case and motherboard connector compatibility, and consider a modular design for better cable management.

6. Redundant Power Supplies
  
  Redundant power supplies provide a backup unit that keeps a system running if the primary PSU fails — mainly relevant to enterprise
  servers and high-availability systems, not typical desktop builds.
  
  Hot-swappable vs. cold-swappable: a hot-swappable PSU can be replaced while the system keeps running (slides out like a drawer); a
  cold-swappable PSU requires shutting the system down first.
  
  N+1 redundancy: adds exactly one extra unit beyond what's needed (e.g., a system needing 3 PSUs gets a 4th as backup/standby, or all
  4 share the load).
  
  2N redundancy: doubles the total number of units (e.g., a system needing 3 PSUs gets 6 total, so every unit has its own dedicated backup).
  
  Benefits: high availability (service continues if one unit fails), improved load balancing (distributing load across multiple active
  units, e.g., 50/50 across two), and hot-swappable replacement without service disruption.
  
  Implementation: confirm compatibility with the system and any power distribution infrastructure, and choose appropriate wattage. Best
  practices: use qualified personnel for installation, regularly test redundancy by simulating failure scenarios, and keep
  firmware/monitoring software updated.
  
  Troubleshooting: power imbalance → check that units are properly sharing the load; failure alerts from monitoring software → use
  built-in diagnostics to identify the faulty unit; unexpected shutdowns → verify input voltage and power draw settings on each unit,
  especially after installing new hardware with default configurations.

7. Modular Power Supplies
  
  A modular PSU lets you detach and connect only the cables you actually need, reducing clutter and improving airflow and cooling.
  
  Fully modular: all cables detachable — maximum flexibility and cleanest builds. Semi-modular: essential cables (like the 24-pin
  motherboard connector) are fixed; others are removable and can be added later if the system grows. Non-modular: all cables permanently
  attached — the standard for most off-the-shelf desktop systems; unused cables are simply left tucked away, which can interfere with airflow.
  
  Selection considerations: same fundamentals as any PSU — adequate wattage for CPU/GPU/peripherals, 80 PLUS Gold or higher efficiency, and
  confirmed case/motherboard compatibility. Avoid insufficient wattage for future needs, low-quality brands with unstable power delivery,
  and missing connector types.
  
  Installation best practices: mount and screw the PSU securely into the case, connect only the cables actually needed, route cables
  efficiently without blocking fans, store spare modular cables in a pouch or the case's dedicated storage area, then power on and test for
  stability.
  
  Cable management tips: use shorter cables for a cleaner build, avoid excessive bending (can eventually damage the cable over time), and
  verify grounding is intact (usually wired internally by default).

8. Power Supply Issue Troubleshooting
  
  Common causes of PSU failure: electrical surges or fluctuations, overheating from poor ventilation, aging components, poor build
  quality, and overloading the unit beyond its rated capacity.
  
  Common failure symptoms: the system won't power on at all; unexpected shutdowns or restarts; burning smells or unusual noises from
  the PSU; random Blue Screen of Death errors on Windows systems.
  
  Diagnosis methods: some systems include dedicated PSU diagnostic software; check the BIOS/UEFI for power supply status; review system
  logs (e.g., Windows Event Viewer, under the System category, for hardware-related shutdown events); run load tests with different
  applications to try to isolate what triggers the issue.
  
  Fixing common issues:
    
    - No power at all: check the PSU's physical on/off switch on the back (can get bumped, especially after moving the system), check for
      loose or disconnected cables (especially with modular PSUs, which have connectors at both ends), and verify the case's power button
      itself isn't damaged or disconnected.
    - Intermittent shutdowns: ensure proper ventilation and check that the PSU's internal fan is running, confirm sufficient wattage
      (especially after adding new components), and inspect for loose or damaged connections.
    - Noise and/or overheating: clean dust from fans, replace failed fans (including the one inside the PSU — this is generally the only
      serviceable internal component), and avoid overloading the unit with too many devices.
  
  Preventing failures: buy a high-quality unit from a reputable brand, keep it ventilated and dust-free, avoid cheap power strips without
  surge protection, use a UPS for stable and conditioned power, ensure sufficient wattage with room for future upgrades, and avoid
  unnecessary overclocking, which increases power draw and heat.
  
  A failed or failing PSU is generally not worth repairing beyond replacing its internal fan — a full replacement unit is inexpensive and
  straightforward.

9. System Cooling
  
  Keeping a system cool prevents thermal throttling (the CPU intentionally reduces its clock speed once it hits a critical temperature to
  avoid damage, which also reduces performance), extends component lifespan, and ensures stable performance under heavy load.
  
  Air cooling uses heat sinks (metal components with fins that increase surface area for better heat dissipation) combined with fans.
    
    - Stock cooling: included with the CPU, generally sufficient for standard workloads.
    - Aftermarket cooling: larger heat sinks and improved airflow, better suited to demanding workloads.
    - Advantages: more affordable, easier to install and maintain, and more reliable since a fan is really the only part that can fail.
    - Best practice: ensure proper case airflow and regularly clean dust from fans and heat sink fins.
    
  Liquid cooling uses a liquid coolant to dissipate heat.
    
    - All-in-one (AIO) coolers: prebuilt units with pump, radiator, and coolant included — the liquid-cooling equivalent of stock air cooling.
    - Custom liquid cooling loops: more advanced setups for very high-performance systems.
    - Advantages: more effective heat dissipation for demanding systems (gaming rigs, high-end CPUs/GPUs) and quieter operation than
      multiple fans, since only the pump generates real noise.
    - Considerations: the pump has moving parts that may need eventual replacement, and there's a risk of leaks if not properly installed
      and maintained.
  
  Thermal paste fills microscopic gaps between the CPU and heat sink to improve heat transfer — apply only a pea-sized dot in the center
  of the CPU (it spreads under the heat sink's pressure); too much can cause a mess and doesn't improve performance.
  
  Heat sink materials: copper offers better thermal conductivity than aluminum (and costs more); larger heat sinks provide more surface
  area and better dissipation, within the space the case allows.
  
  Temperature monitoring: available through the BIOS/UEFI (requires a reboot to access) or third-party software (often bundled with
  aftermarket cooling solutions), which can monitor temperature and control fan speeds without rebooting.
  
  Best practices: maintain positive airflow with more intake than exhaust to pull adequate air in, distribute case fans across multiple
  locations rather than clustering them on one side, and periodically check and reapply thermal paste as it hardens and cracks over time.

10. Configuring a System for Cooling
  
  Common causes of overheating: blocked airflow from dust accumulation (worse in homes with pets), degraded or improperly applied thermal
  paste that dries and cracks over time, and poor case ventilation or fan placement.
  
  Fan speed and power configuration: dynamic fan speed adjustment balances cooling against power draw and noise (running fans at max speed
  constantly wastes power and adds noise); power settings can put unused features into sleep or hibernation mode to save power; fan
  monitoring/configuration software shows real-time RPM values against temperature and lets you set custom thresholds.
  
  Case and airflow best practices: ensure no vents are blocked by cables, place fans strategically across multiple locations for balanced
  airflow, and keep the system clean — clogged intake vents and dust-coated fans are the most common airflow killers.
  
  Air vs. liquid cooling — choosing between them: air cooling is cheaper, easier to install, and more reliable (fans are inexpensive and
  simple to replace); liquid cooling dissipates heat more effectively for high-end CPUs/GPUs and runs quieter, but costs more and requires
  more maintenance. Standard office/home systems are generally fine with air cooling; demanding workloads like gaming benefit more
  from liquid cooling.
  
  Troubleshooting overheating: watch for frequent crashes or unexpected shutdowns, monitoring software showing temperatures above roughly
  85°C for the CPU (approaching the overheating range), fans running constantly at maximum speed without the temperature dropping
  (possible fan failure elsewhere or blocked intake vents). Fixes include improving airflow and ventilation, cleaning out dust, replacing
  failed fans (or upgrading to an aftermarket cooling solution if stock cooling proves inadequate), and reapplying thermal paste — generally
  recommended every one to two years depending on system heat load.

Quick Exam-Focus Reminders
  
  - 80 PLUS efficiency tiers, lowest to highest: Standard/White → Bronze → Silver → Gold → Platinum → Titanium.
  - Power rails: 3.3V (RAM, chipset, M.2 SSDs) → 5V (USB, non-M.2 SSDs, legacy) → 12V (CPU, GPU, fans, drives — the main power rail).
  - Modularity levels: non-modular (all fixed) → semi-modular (some removable) → fully modular (all removable) — more modularity means
    better airflow and cable management.
  - PSU redundancy: N+1 (one extra unit) vs. 2N (fully doubled) — hot-swappable (no shutdown needed) vs. cold-swappable (requires shutdown).
  - The main motherboard connector is the 20+4 pin (24-pin ATX) — the 4-pin segment supports both legacy 20-pin and modern 24-pin boards.
  - Rough system wattage targets: office PC ~300–450W, gaming PC ~550–850W, workstation/multi-GPU ~1000W+.
  - Always oversize wattage rather than undersize — unused capacity is harmless, insufficient capacity causes instability.
  - Thermal throttling = CPU intentionally slows down to avoid overheating damage — a performance symptom of a cooling problem, not a
    separate fault.
  - Air cooling = cheaper, simpler, more reliable (only fans fail); liquid cooling = better heat dissipation and quieter, but pricier
    and needs more maintenance (pump wear, leak risk).
  - CPU temps approaching ~85°C are considered close to the overheating range — worth investigating immediately.
